@page "/reports"
@rendermode InteractiveAuto
@using Markdig

<PageTitle>Reports</PageTitle>

@code {
    private readonly MarkdownPipeline _markdownPipeline = new MarkdownPipelineBuilder()
        .UseAdvancedExtensions()
        .Build();

    private readonly IReadOnlyList<ReportItem> _reports =
    [
        new(
            "memory-leak-report.md",
            "Memory Leak",
            """
            # memory-leak-report.md

            ## Summary

            Potential subscription retention was found when retry logic reconnects after cancellation windows.

            ## Findings

            - Issue type: Memory leak risk
            - File / function if available: SessionCache / ReconnectAsync
            - Relevant code pattern / expression if available: event subscription retained across reconnect flow
            - Why this is a problem: retained handlers may keep worker state alive longer than intended
            - Confidence: High
            - Follow-up file(s), if any: RetryCoordinator, WorkerSession
            - Suggested fix direction: centralize disposal and align cancellation with unsubscribe flow

            ## Evidence

            The rule flow inspected scope entry files and followed calls into `RetryCoordinator` and `WorkerSession`.

            ```csharp
            _session.Connected += OnConnected;
            await RetryCoordinator.ReconnectAsync();
            ```

            ## Scope Coverage

            The agent reviewed all scope entry files and then followed the reconnect path into session lifecycle code, retry orchestration, and worker state ownership.

            - Scope entry files reviewed: `SessionCache`, `RetryCoordinator`
            - Follow-up files reviewed: `WorkerSession`, `ConnectionEvents`, `SessionFactory`
            - Coverage assessment: sufficient for the current report iteration

            ## Cross-Scope Analysis

            This finding could not be confirmed from the original scope alone because the retention path only becomes visible after tracing event ownership and reconnect flow transitions.

            The review crossed file boundaries to verify:

            - where handlers are attached
            - whether detach happens on cancellation
            - whether reconnect creates a new owner without disposing the previous one
            - whether retry completion can leave stale delegates alive

            ## Example Failure Path

            ```text
            Session opens
              -> Connected handler attached
              -> Retry starts
              -> Cancellation arrives
              -> Reconnect path creates replacement session
              -> Previous handler remains attached
              -> Old worker state stays reachable
            ```

            ## Impact

            The impact is not limited to memory growth. Long-lived retained handlers can also:

            - duplicate notifications
            - trigger stale callbacks after state replacement
            - increase debugging difficulty because object lifetime no longer matches the business flow

            ## Suggested Remediation

            1. Make reconnect ownership explicit.
            2. Ensure the previous session is detached before replacement.
            3. Move handler attach and detach into one lifecycle boundary.
            4. Add a regression test that simulates reconnect after cancellation.

            ## Regression Test Idea

            ```csharp
            [Fact]
            public async Task Reconnect_Should_Not_Retain_Previous_Session_Handler()
            {
                using var harness = new SessionHarness();
                await harness.RunReconnectAfterCancellationAsync();

                harness.PreviousSession.HandlerCount.Should().Be(0);
                harness.ActiveWorkerCount.Should().Be(1);
            }
            ```

            ## Reviewer Notes

            Confidence is high because the issue was observed through explicit ownership tracing rather than through naming heuristics alone. The verifier would likely still ask for one additional confirmation around exception-path cleanup if this were a real run instead of demo data.
            """),
        new(
            "performance-report.md",
            "Performance",
            """
            # performance-report.md

            ## Summary

            A repeated full-scan path was found inside a frequently called polling loop.

            ## Findings

            | Issue type | File / function | Confidence |
            | --- | --- | --- |
            | Performance risk | PollingWorker / TickAsync | Medium |

            - Relevant code pattern / expression if available: repeated `ToList()` and `OrderBy()` in loop
            - Why this is a problem: unnecessary allocations and sorting happen on every poll cycle
            - Suggested fix direction: cache stable ordering or move the expensive projection outside the hot path

            ## Notes

            The impact is more visible when project size grows or when the worker poll interval is small.

            ## Detailed Analysis

            The polling path currently performs the following operations on each iteration:

            1. materialize the full candidate list
            2. project into a sortable shape
            3. sort the full list
            4. take a small subset for actual downstream work

            This pattern is acceptable for small collections, but it becomes increasingly expensive as project size grows. The cost compounds because the loop runs frequently.

            ## Why This Matters

            - CPU time increases linearly with collection size before sorting is considered
            - sorting adds additional overhead even when only a few top items are needed
            - repeated allocations increase GC churn
            - the overhead is hidden inside a background loop, so it may not be obvious from request latency alone

            ## Suggested Alternatives

            | Approach | Benefit | Tradeoff |
            | --- | --- | --- |
            | Cache sorted snapshot | Reduces repeated sort cost | Requires invalidation |
            | Partial selection | Avoids full sort for top N | Slightly more complex code |
            | Precomputed priority | Simplifies hot path | Moves cost to update path |
            | Batched refresh | Cuts loop frequency | Higher latency between updates |

            ## Benchmark Outline

            ```text
            Dataset size: 100 / 1,000 / 10,000
            Loop interval: 250 ms
            Compare:
              - current full sort approach
              - cached order approach
              - top-N selection approach
            Measure:
              - average loop duration
              - allocation volume
              - GC count
            ```
            """),
        new(
            "resource-cleanup-report.md",
            "Resource Cleanup",
            """
            # resource-cleanup-report.md

            ## Summary

            Several disposable resources are created under failure paths without a matching cleanup guarantee.

            ## Findings

            1. `NativeBridge.OpenHandle`
            2. `ImportPipeline.CreateSession`
            3. `ArchiveExtractor.ExtractToTemp`

            > Cleanup looked correct on the success path, but verifier review flagged the exception path as incomplete.

            ## Suggested Direction

            Wrap the ownership boundary in a single disposable scope and move temp directory cleanup into a shared finally block.

            ## Resource Inventory

            | Resource | Created In | Expected Owner | Cleanup Risk |
            | --- | --- | --- | --- |
            | Native handle | `NativeBridge.OpenHandle` | bridge session | medium |
            | Temp extraction path | `ArchiveExtractor.ExtractToTemp` | import job scope | high |
            | Processing session | `ImportPipeline.CreateSession` | pipeline coordinator | medium |
            | Stream reader | `ManifestReader.Open` | local parsing scope | low |

            ## Failure Observations

            The cleanup pattern was mostly consistent on success, but less consistent on early exits:

            - validation failure before coordinator registration
            - cancellation after archive extraction
            - exception between session creation and final ownership transfer

            ## Recommended Refactor Shape

            ```csharp
            await using var cleanupScope = new ImportCleanupScope();
            cleanupScope.Track(tempDirectory);
            cleanupScope.Track(nativeHandle);
            cleanupScope.Track(session);

            await coordinator.RunAsync(cleanupScope);
            cleanupScope.ReleaseTo(coordinator);
            ```

            ## Expected Outcome

            A single cleanup scope makes ownership explicit and reduces the chance that a newly added failure branch forgets one disposal path.
            """),
        new(
            "native-lifetime-report.md",
            "Native Lifetime",
            """
            # native-lifetime-report.md

            ## Summary

            Native lifetime transitions appear to depend on implicit ordering between managed callbacks and handle release.

            ## Review Trail

            ```mermaid
            flowchart LR
                A[Managed callback] --> B[Native handle release]
                B --> C[Late event arrives]
                C --> D[Potential invalid access]
            ```

            ## Why this is a problem

            The late callback can observe a released native state unless the release boundary is synchronized.

            ## Lifecycle Breakdown

            The native bridge appears to rely on ordering assumptions that are not enforced strongly enough:

            1. managed layer initiates release
            2. native layer marks handle invalid
            3. queued callback returns to managed layer
            4. callback still references released state

            ## Verification Questions

            The following questions should be answered in a real review:

            - is there a hard synchronization boundary before releasing the handle
            - can queued native callbacks outlive the managed owner
            - does the managed layer gate callback execution after shutdown
            - does the verifier see any ref-count or finalizer pattern that contradicts this concern

            ## Recommended Fix Direction

            - add an explicit shutdown state before native release
            - reject late callbacks after shutdown starts
            - ensure the release path waits for callback drain if the model requires it
            - document the ownership contract between managed and native layers
            """)
    ];

    private ReportItem? _selectedReport;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        _selectedReport = _reports[0];
    }

    private void SelectReport(ReportItem report)
    {
        _selectedReport = report;
    }

    private MarkupString RenderMarkdown(string markdown)
    {
        var html = Markdown.ToHtml(markdown, _markdownPipeline);
        return new MarkupString(html);
    }

    private sealed record ReportItem(string FileName, string Category, string Content);
}

<div class="dashboard-page reports-page">
    <div class="d-flex flex-column gap-2">
        <h1 class="h2 mb-0 fw-bold text-dark-emphasis">Reports</h1>
    </div>

    <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm dashboard-card reports-explorer-card flex-grow-1">
        <div class="row g-0 reports-explorer">
            <div class="col-12 col-xl-3 col-xxl-2 reports-sidebar-column h-100">
                <div class="reports-sidebar">
                    <div class="reports-sidebar-header">
                        <h2 class="h5 mb-1 text-dark-emphasis">Legacy.Native</h2>
                        <p class="text-secondary mb-0">4 markdown reports available</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="reports-file-list">
                        @foreach (var report in _reports)
                        {
                            <button type="button"
                                    class="report-file-item @(ReferenceEquals(report, _selectedReport) ? "active" : null)"
                                    @onclick="() => SelectReport(report)">
                                <span class="report-file-icon">MD</span>
                                <span class="report-file-body">
                                    <span class="report-file-name">@report.FileName</span>
                                    <span class="report-file-category">@report.Category</span>
                                </span>
                            </button>
                        }
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="col-12 col-xl-9 col-xxl-10 reports-preview-column h-100">
                @if (_selectedReport is not null)
                {
                    <div class="reports-preview-shell">
                        <div class="reports-preview-toolbar">
                            <div>
                                <div class="reports-preview-file">@_selectedReport.FileName</div>
                                <div class="reports-preview-meta">@_selectedReport.Category</div>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="reports-preview-body report-reader markdown-body">
                            @RenderMarkdown(_selectedReport.Content)
                        </div>
                    </div>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
